          "audioClip": "scene1_intro_night_sky",
          "autoAdvance": true,
          "changeMeaning": "The night-sky scene appears with Earth, Mars, and labeled ecliptic constellations so the student has a reference frame for observing apparent planetary motion.",
          "narration": "From Earth, Mars normally drifts eastward against the background stars. But about every 26 months, it slows, reverses, and makes a loop before resuming its eastward path. That surprising backward motion was one of astronomy's great puzzles."
        },
        {
